}

^["validation"]{
 hypotheses: [
  "H1 cite --like finds a reformatted or moved fragment by cell and boundary where exact lookup fails",
  "H2 tiling changes the host's resonance in ways the fragment's card does not predict; if additive, the effective layer goes",
  "H3 the boundary list predicts expand failures before expansion runs"
 ]
 negative_controls: [
  "whole-file cite prints the same hash before and after selection inputs land",
  "a pointer quoted inside a string raises no link, diagnostic, or edge"
 ]
}

^["stream"]{
 >>[2026-10-13 18:59] observe — The owner asked whether a selection can be compiled to bytecode and referenced or embedded elsewhere. spw cite and follow exist for whole files: the pointer hashes the text (FNV-1a 64 over the source), the card body holds the form, and the card mislabels the hash as mask. No selection input, no tracked store, and no reader in source: the parser splits @bc:3635905ec3dbeece into a number and an identifier, and @bc#3635905ec3dbeece loses its fragment. Today's embed is an anchor plus ={ ~"file#name" } and spw expand.
 >>[2026-10-13 18:59] decide — The owner asked for a plan and for a reading of source code as a metamaterial. The artifact reads a fragment as composition (content hash, identity), cell (geometry card, similarity), boundary (what the host must bind), and coupling (cite, tile, collapse), with three falsifiable claims; H2 decides whether the effective-property layer stays.
 >>[2026-10-13 18:59] note — A pointer in source needs p12 (a hash kind in the lexer) from syntax-hardware-alignment, and its hash basis should be the one refactor-experiment-lifecycle ?[hash_basis] chooses, so the two plans settle that question together.
 >>[2026-10-13 19:17] decide — The owner accepted all three: pointers are spelled @bc#<hash> and read by the p8 reader, fragments live in a tracked .spw/bc/<hash>.spw, and one hash over raw utf8 bytes serves pointers, refactor selections, and the LSP index. Note for the shared function: portableHash today hashes UTF-16 code units (charCodeAt), not utf8 bytes, so a non-ASCII fragment would hash differently from git or a byte-level hash.
 >>[2026-10-13 19:50] note — Spirit contours in relationship-contours consume pointers twice: rows dedupe by content hash, and a pinned expression is an @bc#hash pointer held as a ground, a watched row, or a slice member.
 >>[2026-10-13 20:52] note — The owner drafted ~#scaffold(reference_internal_page_regions) and wants scaffolds filled by an agent. Pointers name the regions such a scaffold cites; the holes and the fill are planned in cli-sense-reorientation and vscode-authoring-probe-loop.
}

^["open"]{
 # Unresolved questions. When resolved: log in stream, then move to ^["done"] as ~#resolved(label).
}

^["commits"]{
 # Named, in order. Planned ~[name]; landed .[name](hash). Names are never reused.
 .[plan](a124b66a): ".[plans] — fragment-pointers stages selection pointers and reads source as a metamaterial"
 ~[cite_selection]: "&[cli] — spw cite reads --stdin, --expr, and --range file:L1-L2; the card prints the hash as content, not mask, and cite.ts drops the mask-interop comment"
 ~[cell_boundary]: "&[seed] — the geometry card lists a fragment's boundary: slots, cited refs, declared anchors, and placeholder hashes it leaves unbound (H3)"
 ~[cite_like]: "&[cli] — spw cite --like <pointer> ranks fragments by cell similarity and shared boundary, never as identity (H1)"
 ~[fragment_store]: "&[cli] — spw cite --keep writes .spw/bc/<hash>.spw (text, card, origin), .spw/workspace.spw declares @bc, and a lint finds every pointer in authored files the store cannot resolve"
 ~[pointer_reader]: "&[lsp] — @bc#<hash> is read as a citation by the p8 root-with-fragment reader: hover shows the fragment and its card, go-to opens it, an unresolved pointer names the store it searched; needs p12"
 ~[pointer_tile]: "&[cli] — spw expand tiles a pointer inside ={ } as it tiles an anchor ref, and reports the host's measure and resonance before and after (H2)"
 ~[cite_selection_editors]: "&[extensions] — Cite Selection in VS Code and :SpwCite in Neovim copy or insert the pointer, beside Fingerprint Selection"
}

